course: everyday
lesson: 523
title: Kinship care: living with Gran or an aunt
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Kinship care is when a child lives with a relative, like a gran, grandad, aunt or uncle, or a close family friend, because their parents can't look after them. Kerry-Ann lives with her nana in Newcastle. Nana is 68 and gets tired by teatime. On sports day the other mums sprint in the parents' race. «Nana, you don't have to run,» Kerry-Ann whispers. «I'll walk fast, pet,» Nana says («pet» is a Geordie word for love, dear), and she does, and comes last, and waves like she's won. Kerry-Ann loves her and worries about her, both at once. She helps with the hoovering, and tells her teacher when Nana seems poorly.
explain: Kinship care can turn cousins into brothers and sisters. Malik's aunt already has three kids, so Malik shares a bunk bed with his cousin Dre. «We're like brothers now,» Malik says, and they fight like brothers too. Jaylon's Aunt Denise is strict. «Uni, then a job, no excuses.» One night he shouts, «You're not my mum!» Her face falls. He lies awake feeling awful and says sorry at breakfast. «I know, bab,» she says (Black Country for love, dear). «I'm not your mum. I'm the one who's here.»
explain: Names can get muddled. Gracie lives with Granny in Belfast, and her mum visits on Sundays. One day Gracie runs in and calls Granny «Mammy» by accident. Mum's face goes tight. Gracie feels guilty all afternoon, until Granny and Mum both tell her it's okay. Elsie's aunt explains gently: «Your mam loves you. She just can't mind you right now» («mind» means look after, in Ireland). Elsie feels sad and comforted at the same time. When Rhys's mates ask why he lives with his taid (Welsh for grandad), he says, «My mam's poorly» (poorly = ill). It's true, and it's enough. He chooses what to share.
explain: Kinship carers often need help, and that's fine. Imani's grandad can do lots of things, but braids are not one of them. «Look at it! I'm tamping!» she wails, staring at the lumpy mess in the mirror («tamping» is Welsh slang for furious). Their neighbour Mrs Adeyemi comes round on Sunday nights and braids her hair while Grandad makes the tea. Grandparents pass on things too. Nikau's nan in Rotorua teaches him Māori songs, and when he gets a whole verse right she says, «Ka pai, moko!» («well done, grandchild!» in Māori).
explain: Some kinship care is made official. Lexi's auntie gets a letter: «Kinship Care Order granted. The court hereby orders that the child shall reside with the applicant.» Lexi asks what it means. Auntie says, «It means a judge says I'm the one who looks after you now, properly, on paper.» And some of it is just ordinary family life. Tucker's grandma in Pittsburgh takes him to church every Sunday. «Yinz better be ready by nine» («yinz» means you all, in Pittsburgh). «Grandma, I'm bored.» «Boredom builds character, baby.» He laughs, and goes anyway.
fact: Kinship care means a child lives with a relative or close family friend because their parents can't look after them.
fact: Grandparents, aunts and uncles are the most common kinship carers.
fact: Kinship carers love the children they care for, and they can get tired; children can help, and tell a trusted adult if they're worried.
fact: In kinship care, cousins can end up living like brothers and sisters.
fact: Kinship carers who are strict usually care a lot; saying sorry after a row helps.
fact: Children in kinship care sometimes call a grandparent «Mum» by mistake; it's okay and nobody is to blame.
fact: Parents who can't look after their child can still love them.
fact: A child chooses what to share; a short true answer like «My mum's poorly» is enough.
fact: Neighbours and friends often help kinship carers, and asking for help is fine.
fact: Grandparents pass on language, songs and culture.
fact: A court can make kinship care official, for example with a Kinship Care Order in Scotland; it means the carer looks after the child officially.
fact: «Pet» and «bab» mean love or dear, «mind» means look after in Ireland, «taid» is Welsh for grandad, «poorly» means ill, «yinz» means you all in Pittsburgh, and «ka pai» is Māori for well done.
exercise: What is kinship care? | Living with a relative or family friend instead of parents | Living with trained carers you'd never met before | Living in a children's home with staff on shifts | Living at a boarding school far from home during term time
exercise: Who are the most common kinship carers? | Grandparents, aunts and uncles | Teachers at the child's own school | Neighbours from the next street | Doctors and nurses at the hospital
exercise: «I'll walk fast, pet.» What does «pet» mean here? | Love, dear | A dog or a cat | Stop it | Quickly
exercise: How did Kerry-Ann feel about Nana at sports day? | She loved her and worried about her | She was embarrassed and wished Nana stayed home | She was cross that Nana came last in the race | She didn't care, because sports day is boring
exercise: Malik shares a bunk with his cousin Dre. What does he say? | «We're like brothers now» | «I want my own room or I'm off» | «He's not family, he's a lodger» | Nothing, they never talk at all
exercise: Jaylon shouts «You're not my mum!» at Aunt Denise. What did he do next? | Said sorry at breakfast | Moved out to a mate's | Never spoke to her | Shouted it again
exercise: Why is Aunt Denise strict about «uni, then a job»? | She cares a lot about Jaylon's future | She wants him out of the house | She doesn't like him much | She wants him to pay her rent at ten
exercise: Gracie called Granny «Mammy» by accident. What's true? | It's okay, and nobody is to blame | Gracie did something very wrong | Granny should change her name so it can't happen again | Mum should stop visiting her
exercise: «She just can't mind you right now.» What does «mind» mean here? | Look after | Remember | Think about | Be angry with
exercise: What did Rhys tell his mates? | «My mam's poorly» | The whole story of his family | A made-up story about his mam | «Mind your own business, idiots»
exercise: What does «poorly» mean? | Ill | Without any money | Very sad | Badly behaved
exercise: Who helps with Imani's braids? | Mrs Adeyemi, the neighbour | Her grandad, by video | Nobody, she cuts them off | Her teacher before school
exercise: «Ka pai, moko!» What does Nan mean? | Well done, grandchild | Go to bed, grandchild | Sing louder, grandchild | Be quiet, grandchild
exercise: What does Nikau learn from his nan? | Māori songs | How to drive | Russian dances | How to braid
exercise: Auntie's letter says «Kinship Care Order granted». What does it mean? | A judge says she's officially the one who looks after Lexi | Lexi has been ordered to go back to live with her mum | Auntie has to pay a fine to the court for looking after her | Lexi has won a prize at school for good behaviour
exercise: «Yinz better be ready by nine.» What does «yinz» mean? | You all | Young ones | Quickly | Sleepy heads
exercise: «Grandma, I'm bored.» What does she answer? | «Boredom builds character, baby» | «Fine, stay home and play games» | «Then you can go to bed early» | «Me too, let's go home»
test: «if u live with ur nan ur mum mustn't love u». Right? | No, parents who can't look after a child can still love them | Yes, because a mum who loves you always, always looks after you herself | Yes, unless she visits you on Sundays and birthdays | No, because nans are the only ones who can love you
test: Kerry-Ann notices Nana is out of breath on the stairs every night. What's the wise move? | Help more, and tell a trusted adult she's worried | Say nothing, as nans never get ill at all | Make Nana run in the parents' race next year to get fit again | Hide it so nobody takes her away from Nana
test: What would most likely happen if Jaylon never said sorry to Aunt Denise? | They'd both keep feeling hurt | She'd forget it the next day | He'd feel better and better every single day | She'd stop making him dinner
test: Gracie's mate: «u called ur gran Mammy?? that's mad, u've got 2 mums». What's the kindest true answer? | It's an easy mix-up when Granny looks after you every day | Gracie is just being silly to upset her mum on purpose | Gracie should never say «Mammy» again to anyone | Gracie's mum isn't her mum any more, so it's fine
test: In a cartoon, a kid living with his gran is sad and lonely forever. What's more real? | It can be hard, and it can also be warm and full of family | Kids living with grans are always lonely and miserable | Living with a gran is always perfect, cosy and never ever hard | Grans are too old to look after anybody at all
test: Imani's grandad can't braid. What does that show? | Kinship carers sometimes need help, and asking is fine | Grandad isn't good enough to look after a girl at all, so she must move | Imani should stop having braids for good | Neighbours shouldn't get involved in families
test: Rhys says only «My mam's poorly». Was he lying? | No, it's true, and he chose what to share | Yes, because it isn't the whole story | Yes, he should have told his mates absolutely everything | No, but he had to tell the teacher
test: Imani, at the mirror: «I'm tamping!» How does she feel? | Furious that her hair looks a mess | Pleased with how it looks | Sleepy | Hungry for her tea
test: ★★★ Nana is 68 and Kerry-Ann is 9. How old was Nana when Kerry-Ann was born? | 59 | 77 | 58 | 60
test: ★★★ «Tea at Aunt Denise's, no wait, at Malik's aunt's»: tea is for the aunt, her three kids and Malik. Uncle works nights and isn't home. How many plates? | 5 | 6 | 4 | 3
test: Tucker: «church again?? I'm so bored». What would most likely happen if he went anyway with Grandma? | A dull hour, and a laugh with Grandma | Grandma would ground him for a month | He'd never be allowed out again | Grandma would let him skip forever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises kinship care? | The care of a child by a relative or close family friend where the parents are unable to provide it | The care of a child by trained strangers in their own home on a temporary basis | The residence of a child in a staffed institution operating in shifts | The attendance of a child at a residential school during term time
exam: Identify the meaning of a Kinship Care Order as explained in the lesson. | The court confirms officially that the carer looks after the child | The court instructs the child to return to the parents forthwith | The court imposes a financial penalty upon the kinship carer | The court awards the child a prize in recognition of exemplary conduct at school
exam: Given that a child in kinship care is asked by peers the reason for the arrangement, ascertain what the child is obliged to disclose. | Only what the child chooses to share | The full particulars of the family circumstances | A written statement signed by the carer | Nothing true, but a fabricated account
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child who addresses a grandparent as «Mammy» in error. | The error is understandable and attributes blame to no one | The error constitutes grave disrespect towards the mother | The error requires the grandparent to adopt a different name | The error obliges the mother to discontinue her visits
